1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,960
Delayed sleep phase syndrome isn't about being lazy — it's your body clock running late.

2
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:10,320
If you can't fall asleep until well past midnight no matter what you try, this might be why.

3
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:12,960
Three habits keep pushing that clock later.

4
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:20,240
First, bright screens late at night suppress melatonin right when your brain should be winding down, delaying your clock even further.

5
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:22,640
Second, sleeping in on weekends.

6
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:33,920
Staying up late, then catching up with a late wake time, creates what researchers call social jetlag — and since your clock resets around wake time, that inconsistency drags your rhythm later and later.

7
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:36,320
Third, not enough morning light.

8
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:39,600
Morning light tells your clock a new day has started.

9
00:00:39,920 --> 00:00:42,160
Skip it, and your rhythm never resets.

10
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:50,000
Dim the lights an hour before bed, wake up at the same time daily, even on weekends, and get outside within thirty minutes of waking.

11
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:52,960
Those three habits can help realign your clock.

12
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:57,360
If your bedtime keeps drifting no matter what, a sleep specialist can help.
